every rock has a story to tell no matter  what kind of rock or where you find it  it preserves evidence of its history and  we can learn to decipher that history  take for example the Rock and the  Science Center lobby this piece of mica  schist now serving as a wall decoration  started its life long before the  dinosaurs it has undergone many changes  as it has travelled many miles both  horizontally and vertically over  hundreds of millions of years its story  starts about 450 million years ago on  the east coast of the proto North  American continent where rivers float  into the ocean the muddy sediment  carried by the rivers slowly settled out  in the quiet water beyond the waves over  hundreds of thousands of years more mud  was deposited every year gradually  accumulating to form a thick layer  during this time the Aptus ocean was  gradually decreasing in size because the  tectonic plate carrying the proto  african continent was converging with  the proto north american plate the  western leading edge of the proto  african plate bent down and subducted  underneath the eastern edge of the proto  north american plate the presence of a  deep oceanic trench along the subduction  zone allowed a great thickness of  sediments eroded from the proto north  american continent to accumulate the  increasing weight of overlying younger  sediments caused the mud to be compacted  and cemented into a fine-grained  sedimentary rock called shale eventually  the eye Aptus ocean floor was completely  subducting allowing proto africa to  collide with proto North America forming  the supercontinent of Pangea continents  have to lower density to be subducted so  consonant consonant collisions cause  intense deformation of the sedimentary  rocks caught in between our shale layer  was buried even deeper and folded like  an accordion this compression caused the  clay minerals in the shale to be rotated  into alignment perpendicular to the  compression and the higher temperature  at this depth caused the  Minerals to recrystallize into somewhat  larger mica grains forming a metamorphic  rock called slate clay minerals and  Myka's all have a crystal structure made  up of thin sheets of atoms that are  strongly bonded within each sheet but  have very weak bonds between each sheet  graphite has a very similar crystal  structure which is why it is used in  pencils when you write on a page with  one you brush off thin sheets of carbon  atoms when clay minerals and Myka's  become strongly aligned in a rock that  is squeezed the rock itself develops a  fine scale layering or foliation along  which it tends to break most easily much  like the pages in a book  thus the orientation of the foliation in  an ancient metamorphic rock preserves a  record of the plate compression  direction continued compression between  the two continents further deformed the  rocks it formed the very high ancestral  Appalachian Mountains and also formed  correspondingly deep roots this  deformation caused the slate to be  buried somewhat deeper where the  temperature was even greater this in  turn caused the mica grains to grow even  larger in size forming the sparkly  silver coloured muscovite we see today  and transforming the rock from a slate  into a mica schist after the Appalachian  Mountains reached about as high as the  present Himalayas the two continents  could not converge anymore and so the  mountains began to slowly erode about  200 million years ago there was a  reversal of place for adding directions  which caused the supercontinent of  Pangea to ripped apart and the present  atlantic ocean began to open the rift in  created valleys like the african rift  valleys of today and dinosaurs left  their footprints in the mud long lake  shores and present-day Connecticut in  Massachusetts the Appalachian Mountain  has continued to be eroded during this  time glaciers and rivers and landslides  gradually wore them down lower and lower  transporting sediments downhill and  ultimately depositing them in the  growing Atlantic Ocean as the mountain  peaks were lowered by erosion the total  mass above ground decreased to  compensate for this the mountain roots  rose buoyantly upward just like an  iceberg that melts on the top that is  replenished by the buoyant rise of its  route thus over tens of millions of  years the once deeply buried mica schist  was passively uplifted closer and closer  to the Earth's surface  the schist was finally exposed at the  surface within the recent Pleistocene  Ice Age when ice sheets originating in  northern Canada scraped south across New  England multiple times and then only  very recently a geologist found the mica  schist in New Hampshire so get back to  brown and put together it's amazing  story
 

